The frequency of internet service outages reported by customers in Texas and New York is identical, with 671 reports each. However, resolution times in Texas are generally longer and less precise compared to New York.

## Key Findings
• Both Texas and New York reported 671 internet service outages [1], [2].
• In Texas, resolution times are often estimated between one to four hours, with some cases lacking a clear ETA [3], [4], [5].
• In New York, resolution times are typically more specific, ranging from two to three hours, with some outages resolved in under one hour [6], [7], [8].

## Analysis
The data indicates that while the frequency of outages is the same in both states, the resolution times differ significantly. Texas customers often receive broader and less precise estimates, which may contribute to higher frustration levels. In contrast, New York customers generally receive more specific resolution timelines, which could help in managing customer expectations better. The recurring mention of fiber cuts as a cause in both states suggests a systemic issue with infrastructure that needs addressing.

## Recommendations
- Improve communication with Texas customers by providing more precise resolution estimates to manage expectations better.
- Investigate the root causes of frequent fiber cuts and develop strategies to mitigate these disruptions.
- Consider implementing proactive notification systems to keep customers informed about outages and expected resolution times.

