2025-12-05 01:40:01.668 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:116 - ## Summary
Customers express significant frustration with the reliability of internet service, particularly concerning frequent disconnections and slow speeds during peak hours.

## Key Findings
• Customers frequently report both slow internet speeds and unreliable connectivity, with issues such as frequent disconnections and interruptions during use [1], [2], [3].
• The "Plus" plan, offering up to 100 megabits, is often cited as inadequate during peak usage times, leading to customer dissatisfaction [4], [5], [6].
• Network outages, often due to fiber cuts, exacerbate these reliability issues, causing further frustration among customers [7], [8].
• Customers are considering switching providers due to these persistent reliability issues, indicating a potential risk of customer churn [9], [10].

## Analysis
The data reveals a systemic issue with service reliability, particularly for customers on the "Plus" plan. The combination of slow speeds and frequent disconnections during peak hours suggests that the network may be overburdened, leading to congestion and reduced performance. Additionally, network outages, often due to infrastructure issues like fiber cuts, further compound these problems. The recurring nature of these complaints across different regions indicates that this is not an isolated issue but rather a widespread challenge that needs addressing. The consideration by customers to switch providers highlights the urgency of resolving these issues to maintain customer loyalty.

## Recommendations
• Consider upgrading network infrastructure to handle peak hour demand more effectively, particularly for customers on the "Plus" plan.
• Improve communication with customers regarding outages and expected resolution times to manage expectations and reduce frustration.
• Explore offering incentives or temporary upgrades to affected customers to mitigate dissatisfaction and prevent churn.

