2025-12-05 01:46:27.985 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:116 - ## Summary
Discrepancies between pre-qualification expectations and actual loan offers primarily involve differences in interest rates and fees, with pre-qualification often providing more favorable terms than the final offers.

## Key Findings
• Customers frequently reported higher interest rates in final loan offers compared to pre-qualification estimates, with differences such as a 5% pre-qualification rate versus a 6% final offer [1].
• Pre-qualification often led to expectations of better loan terms, but final offers were influenced by additional factors like credit score and debt-to-income ratio, which were not fully accounted for in initial estimates [1], [2].
• Some customers believed pre-qualification would improve acceptance rates, but it was clarified that pre-qualification does not necessarily affect the final acceptance odds [3], [4].

## Analysis
The data reveals a systemic issue where pre-qualification processes create expectations that are not met in final loan offers. This discrepancy is primarily due to additional factors considered during the final approval process, which are not fully transparent to customers during pre-qualification. This lack of transparency can lead to customer dissatisfaction and a perception of misleading practices. Additionally, the belief that pre-qualification improves acceptance rates is a misconception that needs addressing to manage customer expectations better.

## Recommendations
• Improve transparency in the pre-qualification process by clearly communicating that final loan terms may vary based on additional factors.
• Educate customers on the role of pre-qualification and its limitations in affecting final loan terms and acceptance rates.
• Consider revising the pre-qualification communication strategy to set more realistic expectations regarding potential changes in loan terms.

