2025-12-05 03:05:46.904 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:116 - ## Summary
Customers in Illinois are experiencing frequent internet outages primarily due to scheduled maintenance and severe weather conditions.

## Key Findings
• Scheduled maintenance is a common cause of outages, as noted in multiple customer interactions [1], [3].
• Severe weather has also been identified as a significant factor contributing to network disruptions [2], [6].
• Fiber cuts during maintenance activities have been reported, further exacerbating the issue [4], [5].
• Customers express frustration over the frequency and impact of these outages, with some considering switching providers due to reliability concerns [3], [6].

## Analysis
The data reveals a systemic issue with network reliability in Illinois, primarily driven by scheduled maintenance and weather-related disruptions. The recurrence of these outages suggests that current maintenance schedules and infrastructure resilience may not be adequately addressing customer needs. The dissatisfaction expressed by customers, including threats to switch providers, indicates a potential churn risk for the service providers. The combination of maintenance and weather-related issues points to a need for improved communication and infrastructure planning to mitigate these disruptions.

## Recommendations
• Review and optimize maintenance schedules to minimize customer impact, possibly by conducting maintenance during off-peak hours.
• Enhance infrastructure resilience to weather-related disruptions, potentially through investment in more robust technology or backup systems.
• Improve communication with customers regarding outage causes and expected resolution times to manage expectations and reduce frustration.
• Monitor customer feedback closely to identify trends and address concerns proactively, reducing the risk of customer churn.

