2025-12-05 03:07:42.738 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:116 - ## Summary
Agents typically verify booking details, explain refund policies, and discuss rebooking options before escalating issues to a supervisor.

## Key Findings
• Agents often start by verifying booking references and checking flight details before proceeding with any further action [1], [2].
• They explain the refund policy and discuss available options, such as travel credits, especially in cases involving weather-related delays [3], [4].
• Agents attempt to manage customer expectations by clarifying that escalation to a supervisor may not change the outcome [5], [6].
• In urgent situations, agents explore immediate solutions, such as rebooking on the next available flight, before considering escalation [7].

## Analysis
The data reveals a consistent approach by agents to first gather necessary information and provide initial solutions based on company policies. This includes explaining refund policies and offering travel credits when applicable. Agents also manage expectations by informing customers that supervisor escalation might not alter the decision. This approach suggests a systemic effort to resolve issues at the agent level before involving supervisors, which could help streamline operations and reduce unnecessary escalations.

## Recommendations
- Enhance agent training to ensure they can effectively communicate policy details and manage customer expectations, potentially reducing the need for escalations.
- Consider revising policies to offer more flexible solutions upfront, which could improve customer satisfaction and reduce the frequency of escalations.

