2025-12-05 03:07:56.785 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:116 - ## Summary
SkyBridge Airlines customers often receive vouchers instead of cash refunds when they miss a flight due to the airline's policy, which defaults to issuing vouchers unless specific circumstances warrant a cash refund.

## Key Findings
• SkyBridge Airlines typically issues vouchers for future travel instead of cash refunds, especially if the refund request does not specify a preference for cash [1], [2].
• Cash refunds are generally reserved for specific circumstances, such as airline errors, while personal cancellations or weather-related cancellations usually result in vouchers [3], [4].
• Customers express frustration with the default voucher policy, indicating a preference for cash refunds in many cases [1], [5].

## Analysis
The data reveals a systemic issue with SkyBridge Airlines' refund policy, where the default issuance of vouchers over cash refunds leads to customer dissatisfaction. This policy seems to be a standard practice unless customers explicitly request cash or meet specific criteria, such as airline error. The frequent issuance of vouchers, even in cases of weather-related cancellations, contributes to customer frustration and may impact customer loyalty.

## Recommendations
- Consider revising the refund policy to offer clearer guidelines and more flexibility for cash refunds, especially in cases of involuntary cancellations.
- Improve communication with customers regarding refund options at the time of booking and during the refund request process to manage expectations and reduce frustration.
- Monitor customer feedback on refund experiences to identify areas for policy improvement and enhance customer satisfaction.

