2025-12-05 03:08:32.029 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:116 - ## Summary
Customers frequently report slow internet speeds during peak hours, particularly in the evenings.

## Key Findings
• Customers on the "Plus" plan, which offers up to 100 megabits, experience significant slowdowns during peak usage times due to network congestion [1], [2], [3].
• The issue is prevalent in various regions, including New York and Illinois, indicating a widespread problem rather than isolated incidents [1], [3].
• Customers express frustration with the inability to stream content effectively during these times, leading some to consider switching providers [1], [3].

## Analysis
The recurring theme of slow internet speeds during peak hours suggests a systemic issue with network capacity and congestion management. The "Plus" plan, while advertised to provide up to 100 megabits, appears insufficient to meet customer needs during high-demand periods. This pattern indicates that the current infrastructure may not be adequately equipped to handle peak traffic, leading to customer dissatisfaction and potential churn.

## Recommendations
• Consider upgrading network infrastructure to better handle peak hour traffic and reduce congestion.
• Re-evaluate the "Plus" plan's capacity and consider offering higher-speed options or dynamic bandwidth allocation during peak times.
• Proactively communicate with customers about expected performance during peak hours and potential solutions to manage their expectations.

