2025-12-05 03:08:56.901 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:116 - ## Summary
Customers express significant frustration with the reliability of internet service, particularly concerning frequent disconnections and slow speeds during peak hours.

## Key Findings
• Customers frequently report both slow internet speeds and unreliable connectivity, with issues such as frequent disconnections and buffering during streaming [1], [2], [3].
• The "Plus" plan, offering up to 100 megabits, is often cited as insufficient during peak usage times, leading to customer dissatisfaction [4], [5].
• Network outages, often due to external factors like fiber cuts or weather disruptions, exacerbate reliability issues, causing further frustration [6], [7].
• Customers are considering switching to other providers due to perceived better reliability and service during outages [8], [9].

## Analysis
The data reveals a systemic issue with service reliability, particularly during peak hours and in the face of network outages. The "Plus" plan, while advertised to support up to 100 megabits, often fails to deliver expected speeds during high-demand periods, leading to customer dissatisfaction. This is compounded by frequent network outages, which are sometimes beyond the control of the service provider but still impact customer perception negatively. The recurring theme of customers considering switching providers suggests a competitive risk if these issues are not addressed.

## Recommendations
- Consider upgrading infrastructure to better handle peak hour demand and reduce network congestion.
- Improve communication and transparency with customers during outages to manage expectations and reduce frustration.
- Explore offering more robust service plans that can better meet customer needs during high usage times.
- Monitor customer feedback closely to identify and address specific areas of dissatisfaction promptly.

