2025-12-05 03:10:26.798 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:116 - ## Summary
Mechanical issues and severe weather are common causes of flight delays, leading to customer stress and limited rebooking options.

## Key Findings
• Mechanical issues are a frequent cause of delays, often leaving customers without compensation, which increases frustration [1], [2].
• Severe weather also contributes to delays, impacting rebooking options and causing stress due to missed connections [3], [4].
• Rebooking options are typically limited to same-day or next-day flights, which may not align with customer needs, especially for those with urgent commitments [5], [6].
• Customers express dissatisfaction with the lack of compensation and the perceived inadequacy of rebooking solutions [7], [8].

## Analysis
The data reveals systemic issues with how flight delays are managed, particularly concerning mechanical issues and weather-related disruptions. Customers frequently express dissatisfaction with the lack of compensation and the limited rebooking options, which often do not meet their needs. This dissatisfaction is compounded by comparisons to other airlines that offer more accommodating policies. The recurring nature of these complaints suggests a need for improved delay management strategies and customer communication.

## Recommendations
• Enhance compensation policies to include meal vouchers or travel credits for delays, aligning with industry standards.
• Improve communication regarding rebooking options, ensuring customers are informed of all available alternatives.
• Consider implementing a more flexible rebooking system that can better accommodate urgent customer needs.

