2025-12-05 03:12:23.940 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:116 - ## Summary
Customers experiencing flight delays were primarily offered meal vouchers as compensation.

## Key Findings
• Meal vouchers are the most commonly offered form of compensation for flight delays, regardless of the cause, such as weather or mechanical issues [1], [2], [3].
• In some cases, customers were also offered travel credits, particularly when delays were due to crew scheduling issues or for those with higher loyalty status [4], [5].
• The process for receiving compensation typically involves adding the voucher to the customer's account, with confirmation sent via email [1], [2].

## Analysis
The data indicates that meal vouchers are a standard compensation method across different airlines for flight delays. This suggests a systemic approach to managing customer dissatisfaction due to delays. However, the effectiveness of this compensation in alleviating customer frustration is questionable, as some customers express that it does not fully address their disappointment [3]. The offer of travel credits, especially for loyal customers, indicates an effort to provide additional value and maintain customer loyalty.

## Recommendations
- Consider expanding compensation options beyond meal vouchers to include more flexible solutions, such as travel credits or discounts on future flights, which may better address customer dissatisfaction.
- Enhance communication about compensation eligibility and processes to ensure customers are fully informed and reassured during delays.

