2025-12-05 03:12:42.053 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:116 - ## Summary
The primary step being taken to prevent future unauthorized transactions is the immediate blocking of compromised cards and issuing new ones, along with initiating fraud investigations.

## Key Findings
• Immediate card blocking is a standard response to reported suspicious activity, followed by issuing a new card to the customer [1], [2], [3].
• Customers are reassured that they will not be held liable for unauthorized charges, and fraud investigations are promptly initiated [4], [5].
• The process includes de-tokenization of digital wallets to prevent further misuse [6], [7].
• Customers are informed about the timeline for receiving new cards and updates on fraud investigations, typically within five to seven business days for card replacement and three to five business days for initial investigation updates [8], [9].

## Analysis
The data reveals a consistent approach across different banking institutions in handling unauthorized transactions. The immediate blocking of cards and issuance of new ones is a proactive measure to prevent further unauthorized use. The reassurance provided to customers about non-liability for fraudulent charges helps maintain trust. However, the timeline for resolving fraud investigations, which can extend up to 45 days in some cases, may cause anxiety among customers. The emphasis on de-tokenization of digital wallets indicates a focus on securing digital transactions as well.

## Recommendations
• Consider reducing the timeline for fraud investigation resolutions to alleviate customer anxiety.
• Enhance communication with customers during the investigation process to keep them informed and reassured.
• Implement additional security measures, such as real-time transaction alerts, to detect and prevent unauthorized transactions more swiftly.

