2025-12-05 03:12:58.178 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:116 - ## Summary
Delivery delays negatively impact customer satisfaction more significantly for those who did not receive proactive notifications compared to those who did.

## Key Findings
• Customers who received proactive notifications still experienced frustration, but the notifications provided some early awareness that helped reduce the escalation of dissatisfaction [1], [2].
• Customers who did not receive proactive notifications expressed higher levels of frustration and a sense of being uninformed, leading to a lack of trust in the process [3], [4].
• Proactive notifications were sometimes ineffective due to lack of actionable information or because they were not received by the customer, often ending up in spam folders [5], [6].

## Analysis
The data reveals that while proactive notifications can mitigate some dissatisfaction by setting expectations, their effectiveness is limited if they lack actionable content or fail to reach the customer. Customers who did not receive notifications felt more frustrated and uninformed, which suggests that the absence of communication exacerbates negative experiences. This indicates a systemic issue with the notification process, where either the content or delivery method needs improvement to ensure customers are adequately informed and can adjust their expectations accordingly.

## Recommendations
- Improve the content of proactive notifications to include actionable steps or clearer information about delays.
- Ensure notifications are delivered effectively by verifying customer contact preferences and addressing issues with emails going to spam.
- Monitor the effectiveness of notifications in reducing customer dissatisfaction and adjust strategies accordingly.

