2025-12-05 03:13:20.324 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:116 - ## Summary
Fraud alert response times for transactions over $200 are generally longer compared to those under $200.

## Key Findings
• Customers reporting transactions over $200 often experience a more detailed verification process, which can extend response times [1], [2].
• Transactions under $200 tend to be resolved more quickly, with agents often initiating fraud investigations and providing reassurance within the same call [3], [4].
• Both groups receive similar advice on security measures, such as password resets and card reissuance, but the urgency and complexity of handling larger transactions appear to slow down the process [1], [5].

## Analysis
The data suggests that transactions over $200 trigger more comprehensive verification and dispute processes, likely due to the higher financial risk involved. This can lead to longer response times as agents need to gather more information and confirm details with customers. In contrast, smaller transactions are often handled more swiftly, possibly because they are perceived as less risky and require less extensive verification. This pattern indicates a systemic approach where the transaction amount influences the level of scrutiny and response time.

## Recommendations
Consider streamlining the verification process for larger transactions to reduce response times without compromising security. Implementing more efficient fraud detection systems or additional training for agents on handling high-value disputes could improve customer satisfaction.

