2025-12-05 03:13:25.478 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:116 - ## Summary
Discrepancies between pre-qualification expectations and actual loan offers primarily involve differences in interest rates and fees, with pre-qualification often providing more favorable terms than the final offers.

## Key Findings
• Customers frequently expected lower interest rates and fees based on pre-qualification, but final offers were higher due to additional factors like credit score and debt-to-income ratio [1], [2].
• Pre-qualification is often misunderstood as a guarantee of terms, leading to dissatisfaction when final offers differ [3], [4].
• Some customers noted that pre-qualification did not significantly impact acceptance rates or improve loan terms, contrary to their expectations [5], [6].

## Analysis
The data reveals a systemic issue where customers perceive pre-qualification as a promise rather than an estimate, leading to frustration when final loan terms are less favorable. This misunderstanding is compounded by the fact that pre-qualification does not always improve acceptance rates or terms, as some customers believe. The discrepancies are often due to additional factors considered during the final loan approval process, such as credit score and debt-to-income ratio, which are not fully accounted for during pre-qualification.

## Recommendations
• Improve communication about the nature of pre-qualification, emphasizing that it is an estimate and not a guarantee of final loan terms.
• Provide clearer explanations of the factors that can affect final loan offers, such as credit score and debt-to-income ratio, during the pre-qualification process.
• Consider offering more transparent comparisons of pre-qualification and final offer terms to manage customer expectations better.

