2025-12-05 03:33:39.071 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:165 - ## Summary
HarborWest offers limited alternatives to customers dissatisfied with maintenance fees, primarily focusing on maintaining minimum balance requirements to waive fees.

## Key Findings
• Customers are often confused about the criteria for waiving maintenance fees, with some believing they qualify for waivers when they do not [1], [2].
• HarborWest requires customers to maintain a minimum balance (e.g., $1,000 or $1,500 depending on the account tier) to avoid maintenance fees [2], [3].
• There is a lack of effective communication regarding fee waiver criteria and promotional campaigns intended to alleviate fees [3], [4].

## Analysis
The data reveals a systemic issue with customer understanding and communication regarding maintenance fees and their waivers. Customers frequently compare HarborWest's fees unfavorably to competitors like Chase and Capital One, which may offer more lenient fee structures or clearer communication [1], [2]. The confusion around waiver criteria and the high balance requirements for fee waivers contribute to customer dissatisfaction. Additionally, promotional campaigns intended to help with fees do not seem to be effectively communicated or impactful [4].

## Recommendations
- Improve communication about fee waiver criteria and ensure customers are clearly informed about the requirements and any changes.
- Consider revising the balance requirements for fee waivers to be more competitive with other banks.
- Evaluate the effectiveness of promotional campaigns related to fee waivers and enhance their visibility and clarity to customers.

