2025-12-05 03:54:38.567 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:165 - ## Summary
HarborWest offers limited alternatives to customers dissatisfied with maintenance fees, primarily focusing on maintaining a minimum balance to waive fees.

## Key Findings
• Customers are often confused about the criteria for waiving maintenance fees, with some believing they qualify for waivers when they do not [1], [2].
• HarborWest requires customers to maintain a minimum balance (e.g., $1,000 or $1,500 depending on the account tier) to avoid maintenance fees [2], [3].
• There is a lack of effective communication regarding fee waiver criteria and promotional campaigns intended to alleviate fees [4].

## Analysis
The data reveals a systemic issue with customer understanding and communication regarding maintenance fees and waiver criteria. Customers frequently compare HarborWest's fees unfavorably to competitors like Chase and Capital One, which are perceived to offer better terms [1], [2]. The requirement to maintain a high minimum balance to waive fees is a significant barrier for many customers, leading to dissatisfaction. Additionally, promotional efforts to mitigate fees appear ineffective, as they are not well communicated or understood by customers [4].

## Recommendations
- Improve communication about fee waiver criteria and ensure customers are clearly informed about the requirements and any changes.
- Consider revising the minimum balance requirements to be more competitive with other banks.
- Enhance the visibility and clarity of promotional campaigns aimed at reducing fees to ensure they are effectively reaching and benefiting customers.

