2025-12-05 04:00:31.734 | INFO     | __main__:chat_endpoint:165 - ## Summary
HarborWest offers limited alternatives to customers dissatisfied with maintenance fees, primarily focusing on maintaining minimum balance requirements to waive fees.

## Key Findings
• Customers are often confused about the criteria for waiving maintenance fees, with a common requirement being to maintain a minimum balance of $1,000 or $1,500 depending on the account tier [1], [2], [3].
• There is a lack of effective communication regarding fee structures and waiver criteria, leading to customer frustration and dissatisfaction [2], [3].
• HarborWest has attempted promotions to alleviate fee concerns, but these have not been effective in improving customer acceptance of the fees [4].

## Analysis
The data reveals a systemic issue with how HarborWest communicates its fee structures and waiver criteria. Customers frequently compare HarborWest unfavorably to competitors like Chase and Capital One, which are perceived to offer better fee structures and rewards. The confusion around waiver criteria and the high balance requirements for fee waivers contribute significantly to customer dissatisfaction. The ineffectiveness of promotional campaigns suggests that more substantial changes in communication and policy may be necessary to address these concerns.

## Recommendations
- Improve transparency and communication regarding fee structures and waiver criteria, possibly through clearer account statements and online resources.
- Consider revising the balance requirements for fee waivers to be more competitive with industry standards.
- Evaluate the effectiveness of promotional campaigns and explore alternative strategies to enhance customer satisfaction with fee policies.

